IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `lrnt-dates`

-- Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT), Part II -- survival of causes of action on death.
--
-- SEAT: the person whose claim a death touches. Either (a) the executor or administrator of a person who has died,
-- bringing or continuing that person's claim for the benefit of the estate ("Does the claim survive?", "What can the
-- estate recover?", "Does a dust-disease claim keep its damages for pain and suffering?"), or (b) a person with a claim
-- against someone who has died ("Can I still sue the estate?", "By when must I take proceedings?"). A person living
-- with a dust disease can ask the s 6(2) question about their own action. The court gets no seat: what it would find
-- or allow is asked as a fact.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The one duty with a deadline (taking proceedings against the estate of a dead tortfeasor within 12 months
-- after the grant of probate or administration, s 7(1)(b)) is a regulative rule run inside a question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: the whole of Part II: s 5 (effect of death on causes of action), s 6 (damages in actions that
-- survive, with the dust-disease rule of s 6(2)-(4)), s 7 (conditions for proceedings against the estate of a dead
-- person in tort), s 8 (a person liable who dies before or at the time of the damage), s 9(1) (Compensation (Fatal
-- Injuries) Act causes of action brought within the "against the estate" rules). s 9 otherwise only saves other rights
-- (noted, not a question).
--
-- Edition: Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT), reprint REPL004 as in force at 6 December 2018 (the
-- current reprint on 6 November 2026), deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/Law-Reform-Miscellaneous-Provisions-Act-1956-NT-current-reprint-REPL004.txt.
-- No regulation sets a number this encoding needs.
--
-- Copyright in Northern Territory legislation is owned by the Northern Territory. Quoted from an unofficial copy under
-- the NT copyright policy of 8 October 1996 and the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to a court are INPUTS. Answers follow the
-- rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is the text's own no is marked "(the
-- text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording and says nothing more is marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Part II -- Survival of causes of action: the questions of the estate and of a person with a claim against an estate`

-- The endnotes: the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Ordinance 1956 commenced on 28 June 1956 (s 21(b) gives the
-- same date). ss 5 and 8 speak of "the commencement of this Ordinance". A death on the commencement day is taken to be
-- "after the commencement" (fork F1).
`the commencement of Part II` MEANS `the calendar date` 28 6 1956

-- The Law Reform (Dust Diseases) Amendment Act 2007 (Act No. 22, 2007) inserted s 6(2)-(4); it commenced on 7 November
-- 2007 (Gaz G45, 7 November 2007, p 3), "the date of commencement of that subsection" in s 6(3).
`the commencement of s 6(2)` MEANS `the calendar date` 7 11 2007

§§ `s 5 and s 8 -- Does the cause of action survive the death`

-- s 5(1): "Subject to this Part, on the death of a person after the commencement of this Ordinance all causes of action
-- subsisting against or vested in him survive against his estate or, as the case may be, for the benefit of his estate."
-- s 5(2): "This section does not apply to causes of action for defamation or seduction or for inducing one spouse to
-- leave or remain apart from the other or to claims for damages on the ground of adultery." A flat negative: the text's
-- no.
-- s 8: where damage is suffered by an act or omission for which a cause of action would have subsisted against a person
-- had that person not died before or at the same time as the damage, such a cause of action is deemed to have subsisted
-- against him before his death.
DECLARE `Which way the claim runs` IS ONE OF
    `a claim the dead person had against someone else (it would benefit the estate)`
    `a claim someone has against the dead person (it would be brought against the estate)`

DECLARE `What the claim is for` IS ONE OF
    `defamation`
    `seduction`
    `inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other`
    `damages on the ground of adultery`
    `anything else (negligence, other torts, contract, a claim under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 and so on)`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `What the claim is for`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `excepted by s 5(2)` `the kind` IF
    NOT (`the kind` EQUALS `anything else (negligence, other torts, contract, a claim under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 and so on)`)

DECLARE `Does the claim survive the death` IS ONE OF
    `yes: it survives for the benefit of the dead person's estate (s 5(1)); the executor or administrator may bring or continue it, with damages limited by s 6`
    `yes: it survives against the dead person's estate (s 5(1)); proceedings in tort must meet s 7`
    `yes: s 8 treats it as having existed against the person before their death, so it survives against the estate (s 5(1), s 8); proceedings in tort must meet s 7`
    `no: claims for defamation, seduction, inducing a spouse to leave or stay apart, and adultery damages do not survive under s 5 (s 5(2), the text's no)`
    `Part II does not reach it: the death was before 28 June 1956 (NOTHING-kind)`
    `Part II does not reach it: no cause of action existed at the death (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Does a claim survive the death of the person who had it, or of the person it was against (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 5 and s 8)? On a death on or after 28 June 1956, every cause of action survives for or against the estate, except defamation, seduction, inducing a spouse to leave or stay apart, and adultery damages.
GIVEN `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `which way` IS A `Which way the claim runs` @desc Whether the claim is one the dead person had (for their estate) or one against the dead person (against their estate)
      `the kind` IS A `What the claim is for` @desc What the claim is for
      `existed at the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the cause of action had arisen by the time of the death (everything needed to sue had happened, including the damage)
      `liable person died first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a claim against the dead person: whether the damage came from their act or omission but was suffered only at or after their death (answer no for a claim the dead person had)
GIVETH A `Does the claim survive the death`
`s 5 -- does the claim survive the death` `died on` `which way` `the kind` `existed at the death` `liable person died first` MEANS
    IF `died on` `falls before` `the commencement of Part II`
    THEN `Part II does not reach it: the death was before 28 June 1956 (NOTHING-kind)`
    ELSE IF `excepted by s 5(2)` `the kind`
         THEN `no: claims for defamation, seduction, inducing a spouse to leave or stay apart, and adultery damages do not survive under s 5 (s 5(2), the text's no)`
         ELSE IF `which way` EQUALS `a claim the dead person had against someone else (it would benefit the estate)`
              THEN IF `existed at the death`
                   THEN `yes: it survives for the benefit of the dead person's estate (s 5(1)); the executor or administrator may bring or continue it, with damages limited by s 6`
                   ELSE `Part II does not reach it: no cause of action existed at the death (NOTHING-kind)`
              ELSE IF `existed at the death`
                   THEN `yes: it survives against the dead person's estate (s 5(1)); proceedings in tort must meet s 7`
                   ELSE IF `liable person died first`
                        THEN `yes: s 8 treats it as having existed against the person before their death, so it survives against the estate (s 5(1), s 8); proceedings in tort must meet s 7`
                        ELSE `Part II does not reach it: no cause of action existed at the death (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `s 6(2) to (4) -- Dust disease: damages for pain and suffering after death`

-- s 6(1)(c)(ii): where the death was caused by the act or omission giving rise to the cause of action, the estate's
-- damages "do not include damages for the pain or suffering of that person or for any bodily or mental harm suffered by
-- him or for the curtailment of his expectation of life".
-- s 6(2): "However, if a person commences an action for damages for personal injury arising from a dust disease and dies
-- as a result of the disease before the action is determined, the exclusion ... (subsection (1)(c)(ii)) does not apply."
-- s 6(3): "Subsection (2) extends to an action that remains undetermined at the date of commencement of that subsection
-- even though the (former) plaintiff has, as at that date, already died."
-- s 6(4): "dust disease means a pathological condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum that is attributable to dust."
-- A "means" definition: when an element fails, the text's no. The "if" of s 6(2) failing is the text's no.

GIVEN `lungs pleura or peritoneum` IS A BOOLEAN, `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a dust disease` `lungs pleura or peritoneum` `attributable to dust` IF
        `lungs pleura or peritoneum`
    AND `attributable to dust`

-- "dies ... before the action is determined": the death is on a day before the day of determination, or the action has
-- not been determined. A death on the day of determination is not "before" it (fork F2).
GIVEN `died on` IS A DATE, `determined` IS A BOOLEAN, `determined on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `died before the action was determined` `died on` `determined` `determined on` IF
        NOT `determined`
    OR  `died on` `falls before` `determined on`

-- s 6(3): "remains undetermined at the date of commencement": not determined before 7 November 2007 (fork F3).
GIVEN `determined` IS A BOOLEAN, `determined on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `undetermined at 7 November 2007` `determined` `determined on` IF
        NOT `determined`
    OR  NOT (`determined on` `falls before` `the commencement of s 6(2)`)

DECLARE `Does the dust-disease rule keep damages for pain and suffering` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the estate keeps damages for pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm and curtailment of expectation of life (s 6(2))`
    `yes: s 6(3) extends s 6(2) to this action, which was still undetermined on 7 November 2007 though the plaintiff had died before then; the estate keeps those damages`
    `the rule is not needed: the death was not caused by the wrong, so s 6(1)(c)(ii) does not exclude those damages`
    `no: the condition is not a dust disease (a pathological condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum attributable to dust) (s 6(4), the text's no); s 6(1)(c)(ii) excludes those damages`
    `no: s 6(2) needs the person to have commenced the action themselves, to have died as a result of the disease, and to have died before the action was determined (the text's no: its "if" fails); s 6(1)(c)(ii) excludes those damages`
    `no: the person died before 7 November 2007 and the action had been determined before that day (s 6(3), the text's no); s 6(1)(c)(ii) excludes those damages`

GIVEN `death caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN, `lungs pleura or peritoneum` IS A BOOLEAN, `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN, `commenced before death` IS A BOOLEAN, `died of the disease` IS A BOOLEAN, `died on` IS A DATE, `determined` IS A BOOLEAN, `determined on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Does the dust-disease rule keep damages for pain and suffering`
`the s 6(2) answer` `death caused by the wrong` `lungs pleura or peritoneum` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of the disease` `died on` `determined` `determined on` MEANS
    IF NOT `death caused by the wrong`
    THEN `the rule is not needed: the death was not caused by the wrong, so s 6(1)(c)(ii) does not exclude those damages`
    ELSE IF NOT (`a dust disease` `lungs pleura or peritoneum` `attributable to dust`)
         THEN `no: the condition is not a dust disease (a pathological condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum attributable to dust) (s 6(4), the text's no); s 6(1)(c)(ii) excludes those damages`
         ELSE IF NOT (`commenced before death` AND `died of the disease` AND (`died before the action was determined` `died on` `determined` `determined on`))
              THEN `no: s 6(2) needs the person to have commenced the action themselves, to have died as a result of the disease, and to have died before the action was determined (the text's no: its "if" fails); s 6(1)(c)(ii) excludes those damages`
              ELSE IF NOT (`died on` `falls before` `the commencement of s 6(2)`)
                   THEN `yes: the estate keeps damages for pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm and curtailment of expectation of life (s 6(2))`
                   ELSE IF `undetermined at 7 November 2007` `determined` `determined on`
                        THEN `yes: s 6(3) extends s 6(2) to this action, which was still undetermined on 7 November 2007 though the plaintiff had died before then; the estate keeps those damages`
                        ELSE `no: the person died before 7 November 2007 and the action had been determined before that day (s 6(3), the text's no); s 6(1)(c)(ii) excludes those damages`

@export If a person with a dust disease (asbestosis, mesothelioma, silicosis and the like) dies of it before their damages action is decided, can their estate still recover damages for their pain and suffering (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 6(2) to (4))? Yes, if they commenced the action themselves and died of the disease before it was determined.
GIVEN `death caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by the act or omission the claim is about (for example, the exposure to dust)
      `lungs pleura or peritoneum` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the condition is a pathological condition of the lungs, the pleura (lining of the lungs) or the peritoneum (lining of the abdomen)
      `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the condition is attributable to dust (for example asbestos or silica dust)
      `commenced before death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person commenced the action for damages for the personal injury themselves, before they died
      `died of the disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died as a result of the disease
      `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the action has been determined (decided by judgment)
      `determined on` IS A DATE @desc The day the action was determined (if it has not been, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A `Does the dust-disease rule keep damages for pain and suffering`
`s 6(2) -- does a dust-disease claim keep its damages for pain and suffering` `death caused by the wrong` `lungs pleura or peritoneum` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of the disease` `died on` `determined` `determined on` MEANS
    `the s 6(2) answer` `death caused by the wrong` `lungs pleura or peritoneum` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of the disease` `died on` `determined` `determined on`

GIVEN `death caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN, `lungs pleura or peritoneum` IS A BOOLEAN, `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN, `commenced before death` IS A BOOLEAN, `died of the disease` IS A BOOLEAN, `died on` IS A DATE, `determined` IS A BOOLEAN, `determined on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 6(2) exception applies` `death caused by the wrong` `lungs pleura or peritoneum` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of the disease` `died on` `determined` `determined on` IF
        (`the s 6(2) answer` `death caused by the wrong` `lungs pleura or peritoneum` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of the disease` `died on` `determined` `determined on`
            EQUALS `yes: the estate keeps damages for pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm and curtailment of expectation of life (s 6(2))`)
    OR  (`the s 6(2) answer` `death caused by the wrong` `lungs pleura or peritoneum` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of the disease` `died on` `determined` `determined on`
            EQUALS `yes: s 6(3) extends s 6(2) to this action, which was still undetermined on 7 November 2007 though the plaintiff had died before then; the estate keeps those damages`)

§§ `s 6(1) -- Damages the estate can recover`

-- s 6(1): where a cause of action survives for the benefit of the estate, the damages recoverable for the estate
--   (a) do not include exemplary damages;
--   (b) for a breach of promise to marry, are limited to the damage, if any, to the estate that flows from the breach;
--   (c) where the death was caused by the act or omission that gives rise to the cause of action:
--       (i) are calculated without reference to any loss or gain to the estate consequent on the death, except that a sum
--           for funeral expenses may be included;
--       (ii) do not include damages for pain or suffering, bodily or mental harm, or curtailment of expectation of life
--           (subject to s 6(2));
--       (iii) are calculated without reference to the person's future probable earnings had they survived, and without
--           an allowance for loss of earning capacity relating to a period after the death.
-- The amounts are the court's assessment apart from s 6, asked as facts; s 6 only takes heads out. "a sum in respect of
-- funeral expenses may be included" is read as letting the estate recover the funeral expenses assessed (fork F4).
-- A gain to the estate consequent on the death (life insurance, for example) is not deducted: it is "without reference
-- to" it, so it is not asked.

@export How much can the estate recover on a claim that survived the death (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 6(1))? Never exemplary damages; for a breach of promise to marry, only the damage to the estate; and where the wrong caused the death, no damages for pain and suffering (unless the dust-disease rule applies), for lost earnings after death or for loss to the estate from the death, but funeral expenses may be included. Assumes the claim survives (see s 5).
GIVEN `death caused by the wrong` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the death was caused by the act or omission the claim is about
      `breach of promise to marry` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is for breach of a promise to marry
      `damage to the estate from the breach` IS A NUMBER @desc For a breach of promise to marry: the damage (in dollars) to the estate itself that flows from the breach; otherwise 0
      `damages up to death` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages (in dollars) the court would assess for the dead person's losses not listed below (for example medical costs, lost earnings up to death, damaged property)
      `non-economic damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages (in dollars) the court would assess for the dead person's pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm and curtailment of expectation of life
      `earnings after death` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages (in dollars) the court would assess for the earnings the person would probably have made, or the earning capacity lost, for the period after the death
      `loss from the death` IS A NUMBER @desc Any other loss (in dollars) to the estate that results from the death itself, other than funeral expenses
      `funeral expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc The funeral expenses (in dollars)
      `exemplary damages` IS A NUMBER @desc Any exemplary (punitive) damages (in dollars) the court would otherwise award
      `lungs pleura or peritoneum` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person had a pathological condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum (answer no if there is no dust disease)
      `attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that condition is attributable to dust
      `commenced before death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person commenced the action for damages themselves, before they died
      `died of the disease` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person died as a result of that disease
      `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `determined` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the action has been determined (decided by judgment)
      `determined on` IS A DATE @desc The day the action was determined (if it has not been, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 6 -- how much can the estate recover` `death caused by the wrong` `breach of promise to marry` `damage to the estate from the breach` `damages up to death` `non-economic damages` `earnings after death` `loss from the death` `funeral expenses` `exemplary damages` `lungs pleura or peritoneum` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of the disease` `died on` `determined` `determined on` MEANS
    IF `breach of promise to marry`
    THEN `damage to the estate from the breach`
    ELSE IF `death caused by the wrong`
         THEN `damages up to death` PLUS `funeral expenses` PLUS `pain and suffering kept`
         ELSE `damages up to death` PLUS `non-economic damages` PLUS `earnings after death` PLUS `loss from the death` PLUS `funeral expenses`
    WHERE
        `pain and suffering kept` MEANS
            IF `the s 6(2) exception applies` `death caused by the wrong` `lungs pleura or peritoneum` `attributable to dust` `commenced before death` `died of the disease` `died on` `determined` `determined on`
            THEN `non-economic damages`
            ELSE 0

§§ `s 7 -- Proceedings in tort against the estate of a person who has died`

-- s 7(1): "No proceedings are maintainable in respect of a cause of action in tort which, by virtue of this Part, has
-- survived against the estate of the deceased person unless:
--   (a) proceedings against him in respect of that cause of action were pending at the date of his death; or
--   (b) the cause of action arose not earlier than 12 months before his death and proceedings are taken in respect of
--       the cause of action not later than 12 months after his executor or administrator took out probate or letters of
--       administration or within such further period as the Supreme Court, on an application made, either before or
--       after the expiration of that period, allows."
-- s 7(2): a cause of action that arose earlier than 12 months before the death is not, of itself, a bar where the court
-- is satisfied that, having regard to all the circumstances, it would be reasonable to allow the proceedings to continue.
-- s 7(3): s 7(2) does not affect any other law on the limitation of actions.
-- s 9(1): "so much of this Part as relates to causes of action against the estates of deceased persons applies in
-- relation to causes of action under [the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974]": s 7 reaches those claims too.
-- "unless" failing is the text's no. A cause of action not in tort (and not under that Act) is outside s 7, which says
-- nothing about it (NOTHING-kind, fork F7).

DECLARE `The kind of claim against the estate` IS ONE OF
    `a claim in tort (negligence, trespass, nuisance and so on)`
    `a claim under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974`
    `another kind of claim (contract, debt and so on)`

DECLARE `Person with a claim against an estate` IS ONE OF `the person with the claim`
DECLARE `Step against the estate` IS ONE OF `take proceedings against the estate`

-- The duty with a deadline, counted in days from the grant of probate or administration to the last day (12 months
-- after the grant, or the end of the further period the Supreme Court allows).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Person with a claim against an estate` `Step against the estate`
`s 7(1)(b) -- take proceedings within 12 months after the grant` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the person with the claim`
    MUST `take proceedings against the estate`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person with the claim` BECAUSE "proceedings in tort against the estate not taken within 12 months after probate or letters of administration were taken out, or the further period the Supreme Court allowed (s 7(1)(b))"

-- Runs the rule over what happened. FULFILLED means in time. It takes no DEONTIC from outside and is not exported.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 7 -- taking proceedings on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 7(1)(b) -- take proceedings within 12 months after the grant` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the person with the claim` `take proceedings against the estate` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- The last day: 12 months after the grant, or the end of a further period the Supreme Court has allowed, whichever is
-- later (a further period extends; fork F8).
GIVEN `granted on` IS A DATE, `further period allowed` IS A BOOLEAN, `further period ends on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the s 7 last day` `granted on` `further period allowed` `further period ends on` MEANS
    IF `further period allowed` AND ((`12 months after` `granted on`) `falls before` `further period ends on`)
    THEN `further period ends on`
    ELSE `12 months after` `granted on`

-- "arose not earlier than 12 months before his death".
GIVEN `arose on` IS A DATE, `died on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `arose within the 12 months before the death` `arose on` `died on` IF
    NOT (`arose on` `falls before` (`12 months before` `died on`))

@export By when must I take proceedings in tort against the estate of the person who wronged me, now that they have died (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 7(1)(b))? Not later than 12 months after their executor or administrator took out probate or letters of administration, or within any further period the Supreme Court allows. No date until the grant is taken out.
GIVEN `granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the dead person's executor or administrator has taken out probate or letters of administration
      `granted on` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were taken out (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `further period allowed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has allowed a further period to take proceedings
      `further period ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the further period the Supreme Court allowed (if none, any date; it is not used)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 7 -- by when must I take proceedings against the estate` `granted` `granted on` `further period allowed` `further period ends on` MEANS
    IF `granted`
    THEN JUST (`the s 7 last day` `granted on` `further period allowed` `further period ends on`)
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Can I take proceedings against the estate` IS ONE OF
    `yes: proceedings against the person were pending when they died (s 7(1)(a))`
    `yes: you took proceedings in time, within 12 months after the grant of probate or administration or the further period the Supreme Court allowed (s 7(1)(b))`
    `still in time: you have not yet taken proceedings, and the last day (12 months after the grant, or the further period allowed) has not passed (s 7(1)(b))`
    `still in time: probate or letters of administration have not been taken out, so the 12 months has not begun (s 7(1)(b))`
    `out of time (s 7(1)(b), the text's no): the Supreme Court may still allow a further period, on an application made before or after the period ends`
    `no: the cause of action arose more than 12 months before the death and the court is not satisfied it is reasonable to let the proceedings continue (s 7(1)(b), s 7(2), the text's no)`
    `s 7 sets no condition: the claim is not in tort or under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NOTHING-kind); other limitation laws still apply`

@export Can I still bring or continue a claim against the estate of the person who wronged me, now that they have died (Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1956 (NT) s 7 and s 9(1))? A claim in tort (or under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act) against an estate can go on only if proceedings were pending at the death, or the cause of action arose no earlier than 12 months before the death (or the court finds it reasonable to allow it) and proceedings are taken within 12 months after probate or administration is granted, or a further period the Supreme Court allows. Assumes the claim survives (see s 5).
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `The kind of claim against the estate` @desc What kind of claim you have against the dead person
      `pending at death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had proceedings on this claim against the person under way when they died
      `arose on` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action arose (usually the day you suffered the damage)
      `died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the person died
      `court finds it reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that, in all the circumstances, it would be reasonable to allow the proceedings to continue (only matters if the cause of action arose more than 12 months before the death)
      `granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the dead person's executor or administrator has taken out probate or letters of administration
      `granted on` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration were taken out (if not yet, any date; it is not used)
      `further period allowed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has allowed a further period to take proceedings
      `further period ends on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the further period the Supreme Court allowed (if none, any date; it is not used)
      `taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have taken proceedings against the estate on the claim
      `taken on` IS A DATE @desc The day you took them (if you have not, any date; it is not used)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Can I take proceedings against the estate`
`s 7 -- can I take proceedings against the estate` `the kind` `pending at death` `arose on` `died on` `court finds it reasonable` `granted` `granted on` `further period allowed` `further period ends on` `taken` `taken on` `today` MEANS
    IF `the kind` EQUALS `another kind of claim (contract, debt and so on)`
    THEN `s 7 sets no condition: the claim is not in tort or under the Compensation (Fatal Injuries) Act 1974 (NOTHING-kind); other limitation laws still apply`
    ELSE IF `pending at death`
         THEN `yes: proceedings against the person were pending when they died (s 7(1)(a))`
         ELSE IF NOT ((`arose within the 12 months before the death` `arose on` `died on`) OR `court finds it reasonable`)
              THEN `no: the cause of action arose more than 12 months before the death and the court is not satisfied it is reasonable to let the proceedings continue (s 7(1)(b), s 7(2), the text's no)`
              ELSE IF NOT `granted`
                   THEN IF `taken`
                        THEN `yes: you took proceedings in time, within 12 months after the grant of probate or administration or the further period the Supreme Court allowed (s 7(1)(b))`
                        ELSE `still in time: probate or letters of administration have not been taken out, so the 12 months has not begun (s 7(1)(b))`
                   ELSE IF `taken`
                        THEN IF `s 7 -- taking proceedings on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day taken`
                             THEN `yes: you took proceedings in time, within 12 months after the grant of probate or administration or the further period the Supreme Court allowed (s 7(1)(b))`
                             ELSE `out of time (s 7(1)(b), the text's no): the Supreme Court may still allow a further period, on an application made before or after the period ends`
                        ELSE IF `the last day` `falls before` `today`
                             THEN `out of time (s 7(1)(b), the text's no): the Supreme Court may still allow a further period, on an application made before or after the period ends`
                             ELSE `still in time: you have not yet taken proceedings, and the last day (12 months after the grant, or the further period allowed) has not passed (s 7(1)(b))`
    WHERE
        `the last day` MEANS `the s 7 last day` `granted on` `further period allowed` `further period ends on`
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `granted on` `to` `the last day`
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `granted on` `to` `taken on`
        -- Proceedings taken before the grant are counted as taken on day 0 (in time). Fork F9.
        `the day taken` MEANS IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `the raw day`
